WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.046
Commas and FANBOYS

00:00:02.426 --> 00:00:07.329
Comma + FANBOYS joins two sentences. A comma alone never does.

00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:09.620
How to spot it

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.303
Commas moving around and, but, so

00:00:12.853 --> 00:00:14.681
Or around however, therefore

00:00:15.231 --> 00:00:18.955
It’s testing: how to join two complete sentences

00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:20.921
The method

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:24.244
Step 1: Split at the joining word

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:25.898
Look at both sides.

00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:28.510
Step 2: New subject after it?

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:32.692
Both sides are sentences: comma before FANBOYS.

00:00:33.242 --> 00:00:36.007
Step 3: Same subject, two verbs?

00:00:36.287 --> 00:00:37.141
No comma.

00:00:37.691 --> 00:00:39.760
Step 4: However or therefore?

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.723
Not FANBOYS. Semicolon or period before it.

00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:45.368
In a few words

00:00:45.518 --> 00:00:48.125
New subject? Comma + FANBOYS.

00:00:49.325 --> 00:00:50.107
The rules

00:00:50.507 --> 00:00:51.391
FANBOYS

00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:53.536
for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so

00:00:53.816 --> 00:00:56.483
Right: I ran, but I missed the bus.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.921
One subject, two verbs: no comma

00:01:00.201 --> 00:01:02.138
Wrong: I ran, and missed the bus.

00:01:02.418 --> 00:01:04.655
Right: I ran and missed the bus.

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:06.918
However is not FANBOYS

00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:09.250
Wrong: It rained, however, we played.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.924
Right: It rained; however, we played.

00:01:12.474 --> 00:01:14.430
Comma after an intro clause

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:17.295
Right: When the bell rang, we left.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:19.556
Let’s try one

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:24.744
The first prototype of the solar car failed to finish the race ______ the second,

00:01:24.744 --> 00:01:27.689
lighter model completed the course in record time.

00:01:28.339 --> 00:01:33.623
Left side: a full sentence. Right side: another full sentence.

00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:38.783
However isn’t a FANBOYS word, so a comma alone can’t join them.

00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:41.253
And no punctuation at all is a run-on.

00:01:41.803 --> 00:01:45.847
Semicolon before however, comma after. Choice B.

00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:48.116
Common traps

00:01:48.516 --> 00:01:50.194
The compound verb comma

00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:52.901
No new subject? No comma.

00:01:53.451 --> 00:01:54.925
However with commas

00:01:55.205 --> 00:01:56.799
Still a comma splice.

00:01:57.999 --> 00:01:59.445
Commas and FANBOYS

00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:02.332
New subject? Comma + FANBOYS.

00:02:02.882 --> 00:02:03.462
Spot it

00:02:03.662 --> 00:02:06.801
Commas around and / but / so, or however / therefore

00:02:07.351 --> 00:02:08.058
Steps

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:10.077
1. Split at the joining word

00:02:10.357 --> 00:02:14.233
2. New subject after it? → comma + FANBOYS

00:02:14.513 --> 00:02:17.927
3. Same subject, two verbs? → no comma

00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:22.238
4. however / therefore → ; before, comma after

00:02:22.788 --> 00:02:23.513
Remember

00:02:23.713 --> 00:02:26.088
FANBOYS: for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so

00:02:26.368 --> 00:02:29.018
I ran and missed the bus. (no comma)

00:02:29.298 --> 00:02:31.320
It rained; however, we played.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.556
Comma after an intro clause

00:02:34.106 --> 00:02:34.546
Trap

00:02:34.746 --> 00:02:37.523
Commas alone around “however” = splice

00:02:38.273 --> 00:02:41.691
